    "topic_id": 542682,
    "poster_id": 171117,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > be the orthocenter of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" >.</span> The points <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/7/2d7ccd6b9f56d0b35f102159701137050ddc3d87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{1} \\not= A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/1/b61915b1063e10d5eb557046c7d65219eea150ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_{1} \\not= B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"17\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/1/48102605b49bab422f5c082fcc98ffac93b5e8e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_{1} \\not= C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"17\" > lie, respectively, on the circumcircles of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/2/7b2f77cec47dccb4b937145b0d7482d85d246d0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BCH$\"  width=\"61\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/4/024a07fd6fb73bd35772344e0ad9aa7301bcc909.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle CAH$\"  width=\"60\" height=\"13\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/d/4ed19718d84ea66bd08cd0ce70a5a9350d58c6c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABH$\"  width=\"61\" height=\"13\" > and satisfy <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/2/0023c6a664037bd555598305b98ca052634d6132.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{1}H=B_{1}H=C_{1}H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"161\" height=\"15\" >.</span> Denote by <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/8/d2879799e56acffe15ab1f8a96b104cd9e9a4f4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d24174e1d1f578bc7c506e483273b0bd72a9395c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/5/6952e7e9baa8b208eeef695b4c64545220120928.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H_{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" > the orthocenters of <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/e/4be1fa4e84af2d87bffd5ae0c3b2e1f149baee85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle A_{1}BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"66\" height=\"16\" >,</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/c/5ec83d0c2e939656fa86dfa40008753ef0289463.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle B_{1}CA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"65\" height=\"16\" > and <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459a35f284c37a1d80514c4560a49a9dcb6c287a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle C_{1}AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" >,</span> respectively. Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/d/b6d11ea4fb71c88d8bcf5d57c53549412dd33aa9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/c/2cc8fabcf4d90d13670a7341789b0e8e5ba7c9e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle H_{1}H_{2}H_{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"82\" height=\"16\" > have the same orthocenter.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $H$ be the orthocenter of $\\triangle ABC$. The points $A_{1} \\not= A$, $B_{1} \\not= B$ and $C_{1} \\not= C$ lie, respectively, on the circumcircles of $\\triangle BCH$, $\\triangle CAH$ and $\\triangle ABH$ and satisfy $A_{1}H=B_{1}H=C_{1}H$. Denote by $H_{1}$, $H_{2}$ and $H_{3}$ the orthocenters of $\\triangle A_{1}BC$, $\\triangle B_{1}CA$ and $\\triangle C_{1}AB$, respectively. Prove that $\\triangle A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$ and $\\triangle H_{1}H_{2}H_{3}$ have the same orthocenter.",
